WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.220
Numb goes into the daughter that does not hear Notch.

2
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.700
Is Numb an instruction, or a veto?

3
00:00:09.783 --> 00:00:11.163
You now hold two facts.

4
00:00:12.023 --> 00:00:14.663
At each division, Notch decides between the daughters.

5
00:00:15.223 --> 00:00:17.383
And Numb goes into one daughter only.

6
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:19.833
Put them side by side.

7
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.893
At the first division, Numb goes to the inner precursor, which does not hear Notch.

8
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:29.493
At the outer precursor's division, Numb goes to the hair cell.

9
00:00:30.293 --> 00:00:32.633
At the inner precursor's, it goes to the neuron.

10
00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:36.977
At every division, the Numb daughter is the Notch-off daughter.

11
00:00:37.777 --> 00:00:38.817
Two readings fit that.

12
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.530
Perhaps Numb is an instruction.

13
00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:43.950
It tells the cell what to become.

14
00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:47.010
A cell without Numb cannot take that fate.

15
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.520
Or perhaps Numb is a veto.

16
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.100
It carries no message of its own.

17
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:53.920
It only blocks Notch.

18
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:58.930
Then a cell with Numb takes whatever fate a cell without Notch would take.

19
00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.270
Both predict the numb mutant, where every daughter hears Notch.

20
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.330
To tell them apart, take Notch away too.

21
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.120
Which single mutant does the double mutant resemble?

22
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.080
That reading is called epistasis analysis.

23
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:18.853
The test case is MP2, a precursor in the fly's central nervous system.

24
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:22.853
It makes two neurons, dMP2 and vMP2.

25
00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:28.103
Here the Notch signal comes from cells outside the lineage, not from the sister.

26
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:31.867
Notch mutants make two dMP2 neurons.

27
00:01:32.827 --> 00:01:35.007
Numb mutants make two vMP2 neurons.

28
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:39.490
What should a fly lacking both make, if Numb is an instruction?

29
00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.330
And if it is a veto?

30
00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:51.673
If Numb were an instruction, the double mutant could make no dMP2.

31
00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:55.513
A veto has nothing to block once Notch is gone.

32
00:01:56.273 --> 00:01:58.813
So the double mutant should look like the Notch mutant.

33
00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:04.973
In 1996, Eric Spana and Chris Doe found two dMP2 neurons.

34
00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:11.053
Numb is not needed to make dMP2.

35
00:02:11.873 --> 00:02:13.473
It is needed only to stop Notch.

36
00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:15.713
That earns the rule.

37
00:02:16.553 --> 00:02:20.953
The daughter that inherits Numb cannot hear Notch, so it takes the Notch-off fate of

38
00:02:20.953 --> 00:02:21.953
that division.

39
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:24.513
The rule names a side, not a fate.

40
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:29.613
The Notch-off fate may be the inner precursor, the hair cell or dMP2.

41
00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.743
In these lineages, both sisters go on to differentiate.

42
00:02:34.763 --> 00:02:36.863
So Notch is not the stem cell signal here.

43
00:02:37.817 --> 00:02:41.277
Numb needs its phosphotyrosine-binding domain to block Notch.

44
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.990
It does not need that domain to form its crescent.

45
00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.630
Reaching one side and vetoing Notch are separate jobs.

46
00:02:49.633 --> 00:02:51.813
Numb is a veto, not an instruction.

47
00:02:52.773 --> 00:02:54.573
Without Notch, there is nothing to veto.
